<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Durham Castle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1072">1072</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Durham Castle is a Norman castle in Durham, north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, begun in 1072 on the orders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a>. For centuries it was the residence of the prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Durham, who governed the county palatine from it. Since 1837 it has housed University College of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durham-university">Durham University</a>, and with the cathedral it forms a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/durham-castle.jpg" alt="Durham Castle" width="320" height="198" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benjamin Smith · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Durham_-_Views_from_the_central_tower_of_the_cathedral_-_04.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Construction of the castle, of the motte and bailey design favoured by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a>, began in 1072 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a>, six years after the Norman Conquest and soon after the Normans first came to the North. The work was supervised by Waltheof, Earl of Northumbria, until he rebelled against William and was executed in 1076. Stone was cut from the cliffs below the walls and moved up with winches. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Durham, Walcher at the time, was appointed by the king to exercise royal authority on his behalf, with the castle as his seat, and after he bought the Earldom of Northumbria he began a line of prince-bishops.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> were entitled to raise an army, levy taxes and mint their own coinage, which gave them virtual autonomy as a defence against Scottish incursions and internal rebellions. Walcher built the castle as both a bishops' palace and a stronghold, with inner and outer baileys and a keep, and in May 1080 rebels from Northumbria besieged it for four days and killed him. In 1177 King Henry II seized the castle after a disagreement with Bishop Pudsey. Bishop Thomas Hatfield rebuilt the keep in the 1300s, and Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antony-bek">Antony Bek</a> created the Great Hall in the early 14th century, now 14 metres high and over 30 metres long.</p><p>The Norman Chapel is the oldest accessible part of the castle, dated to 1075 or shortly after 1080. Its architecture is Anglian in character, possibly because forced Anglian labour was used. In the 15th century its windows were almost blocked by the expanded keep, and it fell into disuse until 1841. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> it served as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-air-force">Royal Air Force</a> command and observation post and it was re-consecrated in the 1950s. Tunstall's Chapel was built in the 16th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> lost their temporal powers under the Durham (County Palatine) Act 1836. Auckland Castle had become the bishops' residence in 1832, and Bishop William Van Mildert donated Durham Castle to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durham-university">University of Durham</a>. University College moved in during 1837, after Bishop Edward Maltby had completed renovations. Since 1986 the castle and Durham Cathedral have been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> together, and seventeen elements of the castle are listed at Grade I. The castle is home to over 100 students and can be visited only on guided tours.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Durham Castle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Durham Castle</b>, begun in 1072, and the cathedral, begun in 1093, expressed their power.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-palatine-of-durham">County Palatine of Durham</a></li></ul></section>
